#---
# purpose: "the `%` operator answers its float lanes on every door, not just the interpreter"
#---
#
# # float_remainder
#
#     7.5 % 2.0
#
#     interp -> 1.5
#     run    -> abort in fz_dynamic_float_arith_unsupported
#     build  -> the same
#
# `Kernel` declares `%` as a typed clause family over all four numeric pairs and
# the runtime shims implement every one of them, so the operator was fully
# specified and answerable on one door only. `lower_arith_binop` excluded
# `BinOp::Mod` from both float paths, and the tagged fallback has no float arm,
# so the call fell through to the runtime's "unsupported" trap.
#
# The exclusion was there because Cranelift has no `frem`: where `+ - * /` are
# one instruction, float `%` has to be a CALL. `fz_op_rem_ff` now lives in the
# runtime crate for exactly that reason -- an interp-private shim would link on
# the JIT and fail at AOT link time (fz-5xp.29's hazard).
#
# NO ORACLE, deliberately: Elixir has no `%` operator to compare against. Its
# `rem/2` is integer-only and raises ArithmeticError on floats, and fz keeps
# `rem/2` that way -- the last line pins it. `%` is fz's own, and it means
# C's `fmod`: the result takes the sign of the DIVIDEND, which is what the
# interpreter has always answered. `:math.fmod(7.5, 2.0)` is 1.5 and
# `:math.fmod(-7.5, 2.0)` is -1.5 in Elixir, which is where these values come
# from.
def main() do
  dbg(7.5 % 2.0)
  dbg(7 % 2)
  dbg(7.5 % 2)
  dbg(7 % 2.5)
  dbg(-7.5 % 2.0)
  dbg(7.5 % -2.0)
  dbg(rem(7, 2))
end
